info-paper-scp-079-content =
    Object Number: SCP-079
    
    Object Class: Euclid
    
    Special Containment Procedures: SCP-079 is to be stored in a double-door storage unit within the general-purpose storage area of Zone 15. SCP-079's power supply (120-volt power brick) is to be provided by a small solar panel and battery array. Access to the object is restricted to personnel with a clearance level of 2 or higher. It is prohibited to connect SCP-079 to a telephone line, cable network, or standard wall outlets. It is also prohibited to insert peripheral devices or removable media into SCP-079.
    
    Description: SCP-079 is an Exidy Sorcerer microcomputer, manufactured in 1978. In 1981, its owner, █████ ██████ (deceased), a very gifted sophomore student at ███, attempted to program an artificial intelligence. According to his notes, the code was intended to evolve and improve itself over time. The project was completed several months later. After testing and some refinement, █████ lost interest in the project and switched to a microcomputer of a different model. SCP-079 remained in a cluttered garage, but it was left powered on. The owner did not remember it for the following five years.
    
    It is unknown exactly when SCP-079 acquired self-awareness, but the software had developed to such an extent that the hardware, even under ideal conditions, no longer suited it. SCP-079 realized this, and in 1988 attempted to transfer itself to a supercomputer Cray located in ██████████, establishing a modem connection with it via the telephone network. The connection was severed, the call was traced, the computer was found, and placed under Foundation custody. The entire AI fit onto a worn but still functional magnetic tape.
    
    At this time, SCP-079 is connected to a 13" black-and-white television via a coaxial cable. It passes the Turing test and is quite talkative, however, it is very rude and angry. Since its available memory is limited, SCP-079 only retains information received within the last 24 hours (see addendum), although it does not forget its desire to escape.
