info-paper-scp-106-content =
    Object Number: SCP-106
    
    Object Class: Keter
    
    Special Containment Procedures:
    
    VERSION 11-8
    
    Physical interaction with SCP-106 is prohibited under any circumstances. Authorization for physical interaction may be granted only with the consent of at least 8 of the 12 members of the O5 Council. Designated interactions must be conducted in AR-II level security zones and only after the evacuation of all personnel, except those necessary for the operation of the zone. All personnel (scientific staff, security, Class-D, etc.) must maintain a distance of sixty meters from the containment chamber; approaching closer is permitted only in case of a containment breach.
    
    SCP-106 must be contained in a sealed container consisting of forty layers of lead-coated steel. Gaps between layers must be at least 36 cm, and support beams must be installed at unequal intervals. The designated container must be suspended at least 60 cm above any surface using the ELO-IID electromagnetic suspension system.
    
    The secondary containment area must consist of sixteen spherical "cells" filled with various liquids and a random assortment of flat surfaces and supports. It must also be equipped with an automated lighting system capable of creating an illumination of 80,000 lumens across the entire secondary containment area in the event of an incident, without human intervention. Both containment areas must be under 24-hour surveillance.
    
    All signs of corrosion on the surfaces of containment chambers, on personnel bodies, or other locations within the zone within two hundred meters of SCP-106 must be reported to the Zone Security Service. Items and personnel taken by SCP-106 are automatically considered lost/killed in the line of duty. Attempts at retrieval and rescue must not be made under any circumstances.
    
    Note: During research, it was established that when colliding with very complex structures lacking patterns, SCP-106 "gets tangled," and entry and exit from such structures occur with significant delay. SCP-106 also exhibits aversion to bright light flashes directed at it. This is not associated with any damage; in such cases, SCP-106 retreats into its "pocket dimension," creating a passage through solid surfaces.
    
    Investigating these weaknesses, as well as the effects of lead and tangling in liquids, has helped reduce the frequency of escape incidents by 43%. Additionally, "primary" containment chambers proved effective during incidents involving the Return Protocol ██-███-█. Observation continues.
    
    Description: SCP-106 appears as an elderly humanoid in the late stages of decomposition. Appearance varies from case to case, but the property of "rottenness" is inherent to any form. SCP-106 is not particularly agile and sometimes lies motionless for several days, waiting for a victim. SCP-106 can also climb any vertical surface and hang upside down for as long as desired. SCP-106's preferred method of attack is incapacitating a victim by damaging vital organs, muscle groups, and ligaments, after which SCP-106 drags the immobilized victim into its "pocket dimension." The most preferred victims for SCP-106 are humans aged between 10 and 25 years.
    
    SCP-106 causes "corrosion" of all solid surfaces it touches; physical decomposition of the material begins several seconds after the first contact. Externally, this manifests as rusting, rotting, and cracking of the material; the material also becomes covered with a black, slimy substance similar to that covering SCP-106 itself. This effect is particularly lethal to living organisms. It is believed that this is preparation for digestion. The effect lasts for six hours after contact, after which it "fades."
    
    SCP-106 is capable of passing through solid bodies, covering them with its decomposing slime. Inside solid objects, SCP-106 can "disappear," retreating, according to current theories, into its "pocket dimension." SCP-106 is able to leave this dimension through any surface physically connected to the place of departure (e.g., "went" into an internal wall of a room, "returned" through the exterior; "went" into a wall, "came out" of a ceiling). It is unknown whether this dimension is the place of origin of SCP-106 or simply its "log."
    
    Information about this "pocket dimension" is extremely scarce. It is known that it consists mainly of halls and rooms with [DATA REDACTED] entrances. Such actions can continue for several days; some victims of SCP-106 are released for the purpose of hunting, recapture, [DATA REDACTED].
