info-paper-scp-247-content =
    Object Number: SCP-247
    
    Object Class: Euclid
    
    Special Containment Procedures: SCP-247 is to be contained in an enclosure of at least 20 x 40 meters, equipped according to the plans provided by [REDACTED] zoo, covered externally by SCP-148, and located at a distance of no less than fifty (50) meters to prevent unwanted psychic effects. (In accordance with current SCP-148 containment protocols, its use in the containment of other SCPs should be minimized - O5-█). As ration for SCP-247, 18 kilograms of fresh meat is allocated for a three-week period. Feeding takes place in a separate enclosure. Cleaners must enter the enclosure only during feeding; access at other times is prohibited. Video recordings of violations of this order are to be archived and used for the training of cleaners and observation personnel.
    
    In the event of a breach of containment protocols, the wing of the building housing SCP-247 is to be fully evacuated, and all incident records are to undergo strict censorship. The area where SCP-247 is located is to be sealed and filled with gas, after which SCP-247 is to be returned to its containment cell. If this is impossible for any reason, a search party armed with tranquilizer rifles is to be dispatched to the site. Agents of the search party are required to possess highly developed reflexes, exceptional marksmanship, absolute obedience, and a low level of empathy.
    
    Description: SCP-247 is a Bengal tiger (Panthera tigris tigris, adult female) which, under any observation, appears as a harmless cat (Felis catus, young female) with a coat pattern resembling reddish and black stripes similar to that of a tiger.
    
    Remote observation and photography are also subject to this illusion, although it is unknown whether the corresponding image is captured on the medium or if the cause lies in the effect exerted on the observer.
    
    It is not yet known exactly how SCP-247 generates this effect, but it can be divided into two components: first, a memetic effect that replaces the visible appearance of SCP-247 with that of a kitten; second, a psychic effect that spreads over the area surrounding the object (the strength of the effect decreases according to the inverse square law and reaches half its value at a distance of █ meters). Any rational entity within the radiation zone is completely convinced that SCP-247 is entirely harmless, regardless of prior knowledge or experience. Individuals in this area also actively resist any attempts to harm SCP-247, even while being seriously injured by it at the same time. This psychic field can be blocked using SCP-148; additionally, if an attack is necessary, it is recommended to be conducted outside the range of action. The memetic effect is not blocked by SCP-148, so no one has ever seen SCP-247 other than in the form of a small striped kitten.
    
    Usually, SCP-247 begins to purr or meow when a person approaches; it enjoys this and attempts to pet the object (this has been observed even in individuals experiencing strong antipathy towards cats). SCP-247 often enjoys the tenderness of its victims for up to seven minutes before tearing them apart and eating them.
    
    Genetic analysis shows a slight deviation from the typical Bengal tiger genotype, noting possible contamination by [DATA REDACTED]. For all future breeding experiments, O5-level approval is required. The resulting hybrids are designated SCP-247-1; see experiment protocol 247a-14 for additional information.
